@group
    @title=Source
    @brief=The section described the documentation source
    @ingroup=index
    @key=sources

    Read articles below to understand how to write a documentation using docgen markup language.

    Refer to [link=modelDS]full list of documentation tags[/link] for details how to
    markup the documentation structure and complex formatting elements like multi-level lists or
    tables.

    Refer to [link=bbcode]BBcode tags[/link] to learn how to format the text.
@end

@article
    @title=Source code principles
    @brief=The article describes the common principles of the source organization
    @ingroup=sources
    @key=principles

    The documentation source format is a plain text format with documentation-specific tags.

    The documentation markup always starts with [c]@[/c] character at the first character of the code (after any
    number of the whitespace characters).

    A tag is a section of text between the opening tag and special keyword [c]@end[/c]. Each tag may contain
    values, plain text and sometimes other tags.

    An attribute is always one line construction and consists of the name of the value, and [c]=[/c] character followed by the value.

    The text which does not starts with [c]@[/c] character is considered plain text. Following rules applies to the plain text:

    1) All leading whitespace characters are trimmed. If you need to keep leading whitespace characters
       put [c]![/c] character in the beginning of the line.

    2) All consecutive lines are concatenated in one paragraph. Separate paragraphs with an empty line.

    And example of tag is below:

    @example
        @show=always
        @gray=yes
        !@article
        !    @title=My tile
        !    Article content.
        !    Still first paragraph.
        !
        !    More content. This is a new paragraph.
        !@end
    @end

    The tags either defines the structure of the documentation
    (e.g. [c]groups[/c], [c]article[/c], [c]class[/c] and [c]member[/c]) or helps to define complex formatting
    (e.g. [c]table[/c] or [c]list[/c]).
@end

@article
    @title=Define Documentation Structure
    @brief=The article describes the group definition and behavior
    @ingroup=sources
    @key=structure

    The are four tags that defines the structural elements of the documentation:

    1) Groups.

    Groups is the key element of the organization of the documentation structure. All groups are organized in a [b]tree[/b].
    One group is always a root (index) group. All other elements (groups, articles, classes) must belong to
    one of groups.

    2) Articles

    Articles are intended to provide a plain text explanation which does not have any sub-levels and is not
    connected to the programming code.

    3) Classes

    Classes are intended to provide documentation of code-related things. While they are called "classes" they may represent
    many different constructors: classes themselves, SQL tables, XML schemas, sets of functions for non-OOP languages and many
    others.

    Groups, Articles and Classes are only three documentation objects that may appear on the top level. All other elements must
    be used inside one of these objects.

    These three objects must clearly define to which group they are belong to. There is only one exception - an index group which
    does not belong to any other groups. This group makes the root of the documentation content tree and is displayed first.

    4) Members

    Members is another level of hierarchy that defines one element of a class. An examples of members could be fields, methods,
    properties, functions, tags, table columns. Members are always used inside of a class.

    Each element of the structure has an unique key. The key must be unique among ALL documentation objects. The key
    is used to refer the element in contents or in link bbcode tags.

    The key of members typically automatically include the key of the class that contains the member (unless it is directly
    disabled in the class properties).
@end

@article
    @title=Special Case: Class defined in multiple files
    @brief=The article describes how docgen handles classes declared in multiple files
    @ingroup=sources
    @key=classMultiFiles

    While in most cases the content of one documentation object is defined in one text file,
    the class content may be split between multiple files.

    When the class with the same key is defined multiple times, the following rules apply:

    1) The values for all attributes will be taken from the first occurrence of the class. The values from the other
       occurrences will be ignored.

    2) The members of the all next occurrences will be added to the class, unless they are defined earlier. The second
       definition of a member with the same key will be ignored.

    This feature is useful in two specific cases:

    1) A class with large number of methods may be separated to multiple files for simpler modification and
       handling.

    2) If documentation is partially generated from the source code, the following trick may be used:

    2.1) Put auto-generated code to the very end of the source list in the documentation project file. So, auto-generated
         documentation will be used only in case when it is not manually refined.

    2.2) To refine auto-generated description, create a class definition with the same key and put it before auto-generated
         documentation in the source list in the project file.

    2.3) Define only methods you want to refine. The rest of the methods will be taken from the auto-generated part.
@end

